Linda Hersman what gave you the idea to  write about Sandra Day O'Connor and Ruth  Bader Ginsburg so I write books about  social movements so I was interested in  how women had achieved formal legal  equality which is a happy story it has a  happy ending  so I started thinking about Ruth Bader  Ginsburg you can't it's like you can't  think about that subject without  thinking about Ruth Bader Ginsburg from  her time on the court but more  importantly from when she was at the  ACLU in the 70s and til she went on the  bench so was an obvious subject but if  you think about it she was second on the  Supreme Court Sandra Day O'Connor came  to the Supreme Court first and she was  the symbol and the icon that women could  rise to the highest jobs in the land so  she did a lot for women's equality as  well as Ginsburg so I thought well do  they know each other right do they go  out for lunch and shopping for shoes  right no the answer is no they know each  other but they do not go shoe shopping  together but they made an alliance and  affectionate alliance which made both of  them more powerful than they would have  been alone so not only was it the story  of what they did in decisions helping  women to be equal but the way that they  helped each other to succeed Sandra Day  O'Connor said when Reagan appointed her  it's okay to be the first but I do not  want to be the last and she paved the  way for Ruth Bader Ginsburg to come on  the court and be welcome without  question because she O'Connor was so  successful and when Ginsberg came  O'Connor was so happy because Ginsburg  is so unbelievably excellent at her job  that she was going to be another  successful woman just like O'Connor had  hoped so I wrote about what they did for  women and how they acted together to  jewel for women which is a model for  women in the future to know that you can  be allies without being BFFs and how you  can action together make the world  better than either one of you could  alone and then when I started looking at  the cases it turns out the cases are  very interesting so I wanted to make it  for lay readers to be able to understand  how they did a case by case it's going  to be a big term in the Supreme Court  this coming June by June we're gonna  have a lot of Supreme Court decisions  about women and I wanted my readers  whether they were lawyers or not to  understand  how these women did it how they change  the law for women so I did all of those  things in this book did you find a  significant case where they each wrote  opposing opinions it was a case where  they disagreed there were a couple but  but there's one where the struck down as  unconstitutional a portion of the  Violence Against Women Act the Supreme  Court said that that Violence Against  Women Act was beyond the authority of  the federal government to write that law  and O'Connor is always very conservative  remember she's a Republican and she  comes from a Republican president she  was always very conservative on what was  for the states and what was for the  federal government so she said it was  unconstitutional and Ruth Bader Ginsberg  of course said that the Violence Against  Women Act met the constitutional  standards that was one of the very very  very few cases about women where they  disagree they agreed there must have  been 25 ought to gether and they agreed  on all but two of them so even though  one was a Republican and one was a  Democrat and one was a blonde and one  was a brunette a very important  distinction and one came from Arizona  and one from Brooklyn on women's issues  22 cases out of 24 same side so it's a  pretty good record and a pretty good  reflection of what they knew was  important for them to do  did they did they grant interviews to  you I met with each of them gives work  said she would cooperate if O'Conner  would cooperate and O'Connor said there  wasn't enough material here for a book  Oh almost 400 and what's wonderful about  it is that it tells the story of how  Ginsburg started at the ACLU and then  she goes to the DC Circuit and O'Connor  like picks up the baton and carries  women's equality forward while she's on  the Supreme Court of the United States  and then at the critical moment in 1993  gives four commas on to the Supreme  Court and now since 2005 Ginsburg has  been carrying on a torch so I was able  to go back and forth between them  without much difficulty as a writer  because the important the spotlight went  from one to the other in a very orderly  way now we have Sotomayor and Kagan who  are caring look forward in a different  and more powerful way built on the  shoulders of the women who preceded them  so it was easy to write it was an easy  book
 

